{"timestamp":"2026-08-20T22:26:46.891Z","ordinal":24431,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a02148-afeb-7263-833c-99926c5261c5","call_id":"call_yii3XdTwSXec0r7CNQQR0MeK","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\n  \"head\": \"478aa685894c8dacf42e59679b564dcfeb8ef5fe\",\n  \"total\": 16,\n  \"unresolved\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOQ4ERBM6a-lfi\",\n      \"path\": \"datafn/core/src/types.ts\",\n      \"line\": 130,\n      \"comments\": {\n        \"nodes\": [\n          {\n            \"author\": {\n              \"login\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\"\n            },\n            \"body\": \"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Index legacy foreign keys in the Python schema**\\n\\nWhen a shared schema uses the newly formalized `foreignKey` alias, the TypeScript implementation injects that FK as a writable field, but Python's `SchemaIndex` still reads only `fkField` (falling back to the relation name). Consequently, Python rejects insert/merge records containing the aliased FK with `DFQL_UNKNOWN_FIELD`, even though the same schema and mutation work in TypeScript; add the `foreignKey` fallback to the Python schema indexing path.\\n\\nAGENTS.md reference: [AGENTS.md:L422-L422](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions.next/blob/478aa685894c8dacf42e59679b564dcfeb8ef5fe/AGENTS.md#L422-L422)\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\",\n            \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions.next/pull/46#discussion_r3825815965\",\n            \"createdAt\": \"2026-08-20T22:24:06Z\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a01efc-6d32-70b2-a5a3-6d2475cf5417","create_time":1787264806.891417}}}
