{"timestamp":"2026-08-21T04:35:58.523Z","ordinal":25144,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_077ca705e7ea3749016a87d5ab72f48191851a5ff3553da5a2","status":"completed","call_id":"call_Px1oJyHxnQzSTRYEhxCizok4","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/sync-sf-next-to-dev/SKILL.md\\nsed -n '1,320p' /Users/serro/.codex/skills/create-pr-ghother/SKILL.md\\nrg -n \\\"superfunctions-dev|preserved-next|snapshot-local-dev|snapshot-preserved-next|changeset|PR #115|PR #46|019fcc94\\\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | tail -n 120\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":40000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0229a-8394-7dc3-88d3-07bbf870a918","create_time":1787286950.192006}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-21T04:35:58.683Z","ordinal":25145,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0229a-b25b-7563-a216-f894ea5a6b0b","call_id":"call_Px1oJyHxnQzSTRYEhxCizok4","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"---\nname: sync-sf-next-to-dev\ndescription: Inventory changes on local Superfunctions `next` that are relevant to local `dev`, limited to functions and shared packages already present on `dev`, ignoring `.conduct` artifacts and ignoring top-level functions/packages that exist only on `next`.\n---\n\n# Sync SF Next To Dev\n\n## Purpose\n\nUse this skill when the user asks which Superfunctions changes from local `next` should be pulled into local `dev`, or asks to compare existing dev functions/shared packages against next while ignoring new functions and `.conduct` artifacts.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Work from the Superfunctions repository root.\n2. Verify the branch/worktree mapping before comparing:\n   - `git worktree list --porcelain`\n   - `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`\n   - `git rev-parse dev next`\n3. Compare local branch refs `dev..next`; do not compare remote refs unless the user explicitly asks.\n4. Build the scope from top-level directories present in both `dev` and `next`.\n5. Ignore:\n   - any path containing `/.conduct/`\n   - root `.conduct/` and `_conduct/`\n   - top-level directories present on `next` but absent from `dev`\n   - operational folders such as `.codex`, `.cursor`, `.factory`, `.github`, `.tmp`, `docs`, and `scripts` unless the user asks for repo tooling too\n6. Group results by existing function top-level directory and by shared package subdirectory under `packages/`.\n7. Report status counts (`A`, `M`, `D`, `R`) and a short inventory of important files.\n8. Before recommending a pull/merge, mention local dirty or untracked files that could collide with tracked paths on `next`.\n\n## Inventory Command\n\nRun this from the repo root for the standard inventory:\n\n```bash\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport collections\nimport subprocess\n\ndef lines(cmd):\n    return subprocess.check_output(cmd, text=True).splitlines()\n\ndev = \"dev\"\nnxt = \"next\"\n\nexisting_dev_dirs = set(lines([\"git\", \"ls-tree\", \"-d\", \"--name-only\", dev]))\nexisting_next_dirs = set(lines([\"git\", \"ls-tree\", \"-d\", \"--name-only\", nxt]))\n\nignored_top = {\n    \".codex\",\n    \".conduct\",\n    \".cursor\",\n    \".factory\",\n    \".github\",\n    \".tmp\",\n    \"_conduct\",\n    \"docs\",\n    \"scripts\",\n}\n\nscope = sorted((existing_dev_dirs & existing_next_dirs) - ignored_top)\nraw = lines([\"git\", \"diff\", \"--name-status\", f\"{dev}..{nxt}\", \"--\", *scope])\n\nrecords = []\nfor line in raw:\n    cols = line.split(\"\\t\")\n    status = cols[0]\n    path = cols[-1]\n\n    if \"/.conduct/\" in path or path.startswith(\".conduct/\") or path.startswith(\"_conduct/\"):\n        continue\n\n    top = path.split(\"/\")[0]\n    if top not in scope:\n        continue\n\n    group = top\n    if top == \"packages\":\n        parts = path.split(\"/\")\n        group = \"/\".join(parts[:2]) if len(parts) > 1 else path\n\n    records.append((group, status, path))\n\ngrouped = collections.defaultdict(list)\nfor group, status, path in records:\n    grouped[group].append((status, path))\n\nprint(\"Branch divergence dev...next:\")\nprint(subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-list\", \"--left-right\", \"--count\", f\"{dev}...{nxt}\"], text=True).strip())\nprint()\n\nfor group in sorted(grouped):\n    counts = collections.Counter(status[0] for status, _path in grouped[group])\n    summary = \" \".join(f\"{key}:{counts[key]}\" for key in [\"A\", \"M\", \"D\", \"R\"] if counts[key])\n    print(f\"{group}\\t{len(grouped[group])}\\t{summary}\")\n    for status, path in grouped[group]:\n        print(f\"  {status}\\t{path}\")\nPY\n```\n\n## Collision Check\n\nIf `git status --porcelain=v1 -uall` shows untracked directories that are tracked on `next`, check for collisions before suggesting a pull:\n\n```bash\ngit status --porcelain=v1 -uall\ngit ls-tree -r --name-only next -- <path>\n```\n\nIf the untracked files are generated artifacts and not tracked on `next`, say they should not block the branch comparison but may still need cleanup before a merge.\n\n## Output\n\nReturn:\n\n- branch mapping and divergence\n- a concise summary table grouped by existing function/shared package\n- a per-group inventory of notable files or areas changed\n- a clear exclusion note listing ignored new `next`-only top-level functions if useful\n- any local worktree collision or cleanliness warnings\n---\nname: create-pr-ghother\ndescription: Create or update a GitHub pull request using `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`, especially when the current worktree is dirty or based on the wrong branch. Use this when the user wants a PR against a specific remote and branch such as `origin/dev` or `next/next`, needs `.conduct` included or excluded based on target, wants branch/title prefixing, or needs a clean temporary worktree to avoid dragging unrelated history into the PR.\n---\n\n# Create PR GH Other\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this skill when the user wants a PR created with the `gh-other` GitHub profile and the result must be clean, accurately described, and targeted to a specific remote and branch.\n\nThis skill exists to avoid four recurring failure modes:\n- pushing from the wrong branch ancestry\n- creating vague or misleading PR titles and descriptions\n- accidentally including `.conduct` content in the wrong target\n- leaking generated/build artifacts because `.gitignore` was not checked before staging\n\n## Required Inputs\n\n- target remote and target branch\n  - common: `origin/dev`\n  - common: `next/next`\n- optional prefix for both branch name and PR title\n- the current worktree containing the intended changes\n\nIf the user gives only a branch name like `dev`, assume `origin/dev`.\nIf the user gives only `next`, assume `next/next`.\n\n## Hard Rules\n\n- Always set `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other` on every `gh` command.\n- Do not create forks.\n- Do not create extra remotes.\n- Push to the provided remote only.\n- If permission is denied for that remote/repository, abort and report the denial. Do not fall back to a fork or a different remote.\n- If the target is `origin/dev`, do not push `.conduct` contents.\n- If the target is `next/next`, include `.conduct` contents.\n- Before staging, scan the changed content for files that should be gitignored and update `.gitignore` first.\n- Do not generate a PR title/body from guesswork. Read the actual changed files and make the title/body reflect the real scope.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### 1. Inspect The Current Worktree Thoroughly\n\nStart by understanding what actually changed.\n\nRun and inspect at minimum:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit branch --show-current\ngit remote -v\ngit fetch <target-remote> <target-branch>\ngit rev-list --left-right --count <target-remote>/<target-branch>...HEAD\ngit diff --stat\ngit diff --name-status\n```\n\nThen read the high-signal changed files, not just filenames:\n\n- source files\n- tests\n- examples\n- docs\n- package manifests\n- `.gitignore`\n\nDo this before drafting the branch name, commit message, PR title, or PR body.\n\n### 2. Decide Whether A Clean Temporary Worktree Is Required\n\nUse the current worktree branch only if it is already a clean PR base for the target branch.\n\nCreate a temporary clean worktree from `<target-remote>/<target-branch>` if any of these are true:\n\n- the current branch is far ahead/behind the target branch\n- the branch contains unrelated commits\n- the user’s changes are mixed into an old branch\n- a direct PR from the current branch would drag in unrelated history\n\nWhen a clean worktree is needed:\n\n1. create a temporary worktree from the target branch\n2. replay the user’s intended changes into it\n3. use that temporary worktree branch for commit, push, and PR\n\n### 3. Replay Changes Safely\n\nWhen replaying onto a clean worktree:\n\n- For modified tracked files:\n  - prefer patch-based replay over full-file copying\n  - preserve upstream target-branch fixes\n  - do not overwrite a newer target file wholesale unless the file is clearly example-only or entirely owned by the current change\n- For brand-new files and directories:\n  - copy them directly\n- After replay:\n  - run `git status --short`\n  - confirm the staged/unstaged set still matches the intended scope\n\nIf replay causes conflicts or unexpected test regressions, resolve them before continuing. Do not silently ship the broken replay.\n\n### 4. Apply `.conduct` Policy Before Staging\n\nTarget-specific policy:\n\n- `origin/dev`\n  - exclude `.conduct` contents from the PR\n  - if needed, stage with explicit exclusions rather than hoping they stay out\n- `next/next`\n  - include `.conduct` contents\n\nDo not decide this ad hoc. The target determines the policy.\n\n### 5. Scan For Gitignore Candidates Before Staging\n\nBefore `git add`, inspect the full changed/untracked set for generated or machine-local files.\n\nCheck:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit ls-files --others --exclude-standard\n```\n\nCommon artifacts to catch:\n\n- `node_modules/`\n- `.build/`\n- `.swiftpm/`\n- `DerivedData/`\n- `**/xcuserdata/`\n- `**/*.xcuserstate`\n- build outputs like `dist/`, `build/`, generated `.js`, `.d.ts`, source maps\n- temporary verification symlinks\n- cache folders created during testing\n\nIf ignore rules are missing, update `.gitignore` before staging.\n\nDo not stage generated artifacts and then “remember to remove them later”.\n\n### 6. Stage Only The Intended Scope\n\nAfter `.gitignore` is correct and the replay is settled:\n\n- stage only the files meant for the PR\n- verify `.conduct` inclusion/exclusion again\n- verify that no machine-local or generated artifacts are staged\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ngit status --short\ngit diff --cached --stat\n```\n\nIf a temporary verification helper was added, like a root `node_modules` symlink, remove it before commit unless it is intentionally part of the change.\n\n### 7. Verify On The PR Branch\n\nRun the relevant tests from the branch that will actually be pushed.\n\nDo not rely only on tests run from the original worktree if the PR branch was rebuilt in a temporary worktree.\n\nAt minimum, run the tests that match the touched packages or the user’s requested verification scope.\n\n### 8. Create Branch Name, Commit Message, PR Title, And PR Body From The Actual Diff\n\nBranch naming:\n\n- if a prefix is provided, prefix the branch name with it\n- otherwise use a concise branch name that reflects the real change\n\nDefault branch pattern:\n\n```text\n<prefix>/<slug>\n```\n\nor, if no prefix is provided:\n\n```text\ncodex/<slug>\n```\n\nTitle rules:\n\n- if a prefix is provided, prefix the PR title too\n- mention the real primary areas changed\n- do not collapse broad runtime work into a narrow feature label\n\nExamples of what the title/body must capture when present:\n\n- `datafn/swift`\n- `datafn/swift-bridge`\n- `searchfn/swift`\n- example apps\n- docs and regression coverage\n\nBody rules:\n\n- summarize the real change areas\n- mention included packages/directories when the PR is broad\n- list the tests actually run\n- explicitly note `.conduct` inclusion or exclusion based on target\n\n### 9. Push Using The Provided Remote Only\n\nDerive the repository from the provided remote:\n\n```bash\ngit remote get-url <target-remote>\n```\n\nPush the branch to that remote only.\n\nPreferred approach:\n\n- use the provided remote/repository\n- use the `gh-other` auth context for PR creation\n- if push permission is denied for that remote, stop and report the denial\n\nDo not:\n\n- add a new remote\n- create a fork\n- push to a personal repository\n- silently change the destination repository\n\n### 10. Create The PR And Verify The Live Metadata\n\nCreate the PR with:\n\n- `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`\n- the requested base remote + branch\n- the pushed branch on the provided remote\n\nAfter creation, immediately inspect the live PR:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view <number-or-url> --json title,body,url,baseRefName,headRefName\n```\n\nConfirm that:\n\n- the title matches the real scope\n- the body reflects the actual work\n- the correct remote/branch were used\n\nIf the live title/body are off, fix them before reporting success:\n\n```bash\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr edit ...\n```\n\n## Learnings To Carry Forward\n\n- Check branch ancestry before doing anything else. An old branch can make a perfectly valid code change produce a terrible PR.\n- Use a clean temporary worktree when the source branch is not a clean base for the target branch.\n- Replay tracked file changes as patches whenever possible; full-file copying can wipe out newer target-branch fixes.\n- Read enough of the changed files to describe the real work. Titles like “native-backed search” are wrong if the PR actually adds `datafn/swift` core runtime.\n- Decide `.conduct` policy from the target branch up front, not at the end.\n- Scan for ignore-worthy artifacts before staging. This is when `.build`, `.swiftpm`, `DerivedData`, `xcuserdata`, and verification symlinks are easiest to catch.\n- Never stage temporary verification helpers such as `node_modules` symlinks.\n- After PR creation, verify the live PR metadata. Do not assume the first draft title/body is good enough.\n\n## Final Response Requirements\n\nWhen the skill completes, report:\n\n- PR URL\n- pushed branch name\n- target remote/branch\n- whether `.conduct` was included or excluded\n- whether `.gitignore` changed and why\n- tests run on the pushed branch\n- any abort reason if permissions or remote policy blocked completion\n501:- sf-next/next, preserved-next-changes-2026-08-12, PR-43, mdfn, includedUpdateIds, allowRawHtml, insertMarkdownAtSelection, verify:mdfn\n670:- SFN-20, mdfn, sf-next/next, preserved-next-changes-2026-08-12, Markdown, ProseMirror, React, Svelte, Solid, vanilla\n958:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/{superfunctions,superfunctions-dev,worktrees/superfunctions/*}; reuse_rule=verify current ref, account, and package state; counts/SHA/release status are time-specific.\n1106:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/{superfunctions,superfunctions-dev,worktrees/superfunctions/*}; reuse_rule=use the review workflow across Superfunctions PRs, but inspect the exact PR SHA, authenticated account, and clean worktree before applying findings.\n1122:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-10T17-24-09-ah21-pr_112_refetch_review_fixes_and_push.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/archived_sessions/rollout-2026-08-10T22-54-09-019fecb4-0775-7c70-901b-b1c6f5df36e1.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-10T19:22:22+00:00, thread_id=019fecb4-0775-7c70-901b-b1c6f5df36e1, 73 affected-package tests passed; CI billing lock remained external)\n1156:- sf-next/next, git pull --ff-only, preserved-next-changes-2026-08-12, MDFN, @mdfn/core, ProseMirror, CodeMirror, verify:mdfn, mdfn-1.0-dag\n1366:- At the inspected snapshot, nine entries included eight live worktrees and one stale/prunable `/private/tmp/sf-docsfn-core-pack-a2712232`. `superfunctions-dev` was a broad dirty integration stack (377 tracked + 95 untracked paths, 10 commits behind stored `origin/dev`); `SFN-15` held large generated `.conduct` evidence; `codex/mcpfn` had 103 modified + 80 untracked paths. Counts and divergence are snapshot-specific. [Task 1]\n1773:# Task Group: Superfunctions current DataFn and AuthFn audits in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n1775:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=re-audit dirty source, deployment preconditions, and current tests before acting on any finding.\n1781:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-08T03-26-04-CM7L-datafn_read_only_audit_linear_sf_px.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/08/rollout-2026-08-08T08-56-04-019fdf68-06a7-7801-a36b-191690fdedb1.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-08T04:29:17+00:00, thread_id=019fdf68-06a7-7801-a36b-191690fdedb1, read-only audit and verified SF Px Linear publication)\n1791:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-08T03-27-01-wNdB-authfn_current_tree_audit_linear_sf_px.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/08/rollout-2026-08-08T08-57-01-019fdf68-e4b1-7150-a616-6d0649912f3f.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-08T04:36:22+00:00, thread_id=019fdf68-e4b1-7150-a616-6d0649912f3f, full audit and Linear publication)\n1792:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/08/rollout-2026-08-08T08-57-24-019fdf69-3e4b-7470-9b1f-a9a74219509f.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-08T04:24:26+00:00, thread_id=019fdf69-3e4b-7470-9b1f-a9a74219509f, security-focused findings and publication verification)\n1904:- when the user said “commit and push changes” -> commit directly on `main` without creating a branch or PR, after reviewing the coherent changeset. Before that explicit authorization, retain the local-only boundary. [Task 2]\n3067:# Task Group: superfunctions-dev dirty-worktree provenance in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n3069:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=worktree size/origin evidence is snapshot-specific; re-run Git and consumer-log checks before attributing new changes.\n3075:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/04/rollout-2026-08-04T17-11-28-019fcc94-2243-7543-81c9-399c9871de91.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-04T11:50:19+00:00, thread_id=019fcc94-2243-7543-81c9-399c9871de91, read-only 470-path inventory with direct-versus-inferred provenance)\n3079:- superfunctions-dev, Nucleus, AuthFn, DataFn, SearchFn, FileFn, file: dependencies, symlinked-local-packages, root-source-fix, .conduct, 377 files changed\n3328:# Task Group: superfunctions-dev README maturity and function-table cleanup in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n3329:scope: Use for README/catalog edits in `superfunctions-dev`, especially when the user wants project-maturity disclaimers, compact function tables, or source-driven alpha/beta product inclusion aligned to the landing-page data. Boundaries: exact README wording, landing-page statuses, and current table layout should be rechecked against the live files before reuse.\n3330:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=safe for similar README/catalog cleanup in this checkout, but treat current product statuses and final copy/layout choices as repo-state-specific.\n3336:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/07/12/rollout-2026-07-12T15-40-34-019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe.jsonl, updated_at=2026-07-12T10:23:24+00:00, thread_id=019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe, added a prominent alpha-stage warning below the badge block and before `# Functions`)\n3340:- superfunctions-dev, README.md, alpha warning, use at own risk, badge block, Functions heading\n3346:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/07/12/rollout-2026-07-12T15-40-34-019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe.jsonl, updated_at=2026-07-12T10:23:24+00:00, thread_id=019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe, rewrote the README tables to `Function` plus `Description` and validated with `git diff --check`)\n3356:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/07/12/rollout-2026-07-12T15-40-34-019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe.jsonl, updated_at=2026-07-12T10:23:24+00:00, thread_id=019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe, used landing-page `data.ts` as the status source of truth and added only the requested alpha/beta items)\n3366:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/07/12/rollout-2026-07-12T15-40-34-019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe.jsonl, updated_at=2026-07-12T10:23:24+00:00, thread_id=019f55ce-a8c2-75d3-84e8-064914447fbe, stripped first-column links and kept package links inside the description cells)\n4821:# Task Group: DataFn/FileFn transport auth comparison in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n4822:scope: Use for read-only source comparisons between Nucleus DataFn transport auth and FileFn client auth in `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev`, especially when the user asks whether FileFn can adopt the same shared HTTP/auth provider shape. Boundaries: this is grounded in current source, not a shipped FileFn API change; re-check the exact client types and request paths before reusing implementation claims.\n4823:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=safe for similar source-grounded auth transport comparisons in this checkout, but distinguish current capability from proposed API shape every time.\n4829:- rollout_summaries/2026-06-24T06-32-02-7DtI-datafn_filefn_auth_transport_comparison.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/06/24/rollout-2026-06-24T12-02-03-019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56.jsonl, updated_at=2026-06-24T06:37:57+00:00, thread_id=019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56, exact call-site read separated AuthFn-backed DataFn transport auth from FileFn’s simpler `getAuthHeaders` demo)\n4839:- rollout_summaries/2026-06-24T06-32-02-7DtI-datafn_filefn_auth_transport_comparison.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/06/24/rollout-2026-06-24T12-02-03-019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56.jsonl, updated_at=2026-06-24T06:37:57+00:00, thread_id=019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56, verified that the shared `@superfunctions/http` auth provider already exists and FileFn could align to it with caveats)\n4899:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus with sibling validation work in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=safe for similar Nucleus migration QA or browser-bridge diagnosis in these linked checkouts, but re-check the exact dev host, auth state, backend health, and current migration diff before reusing as proof of current correctness.\n4997:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus with linked package source in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev/authfn/core; reuse_rule=safe for similar architecture-opinion or provenance questions in this checkout, but keep claims scoped to the inspected files rather than the entire package tree.\n5008:- authfn/core, services/account/src/auth.ts, social-oauth.ts, oauth-support.ts, package barrel, source provenance, Just answer, superfunctions-dev/authfn/core\n5019:- did you read actual source code, where is the source code, services/account/node_modules/@authfn/core, file:../../../superfunctions-dev/authfn/core, symlink target, epistemic honesty\n5029:- In this workspace, `services/account/node_modules/@authfn/core` resolves to the linked local checkout `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev/authfn/core`, so package reads can and should be source-based. [Task 2]\n5040:applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus with sibling source in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions and /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=safe for similar migration-audit work here, but treat exact risk lists, downgraded findings, and attached documents as checkout-specific.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"t